WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.725
The operating system and runtime stack determine how easy the lab is to operate after the

00:00:04.725 --> 00:00:06.000
first successful demo.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:10.000
Windows can be convenient for desktop workflows, vendor tools, and mixed productivity use.

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:15.000
Linux is common for server-style operation, containers, driver control, and automation.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:19.220
Some users choose dual boot or a dedicated lab system to keep experiments away from a

00:00:19.220 --> 00:00:20.000
daily workstation.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:21.973
The best choice is the one you can maintain reliably.

00:00:21.973 --> 00:00:25.000
GPU drivers are a foundation, not an afterthought.

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:28.890
Many AI runtimes depend on specific driver behavior, acceleration libraries,

00:00:28.890 --> 00:00:30.000
or container support.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:33.990
Once a stable configuration works, document it before changing versions.

00:00:33.990 --> 00:00:37.000
Driver updates can improve support, but they can also break a workflow.

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:41.050
A lab should have an update discipline: know what is installed, why it is installed,

00:00:41.050 --> 00:00:42.990
and how you would return to a working state if an upgrade fails.

00:00:42.990 --> 00:00:47.020
The runtime stack may include container runtimes, Python environments,

00:00:47.020 --> 00:00:51.000
local inference runtimes, model managers, web frontends, notebooks, and APIs.

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:54.000
These components change quickly, so separation matters.

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:58.025
Keep the base system stable while experiments change in containers, virtual environments,

00:00:58.025 --> 00:00:58.990
or dedicated directories.

00:00:58.990 --> 00:01:03.280
Avoid letting every tool install global dependencies until the machine becomes impossible

00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:04.260
to reason about.

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:06.417
Documentation is a practical reliability control.

00:01:06.417 --> 00:01:10.690
Record installation commands, driver versions, model locations, service ports,

00:01:10.690 --> 00:01:13.000
environment variables, and configuration files.

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:16.000
This does not need to be formal enterprise documentation.

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:20.025
A simple lab notebook can save hours when you rebuild, troubleshoot, compare performance,

00:01:20.025 --> 00:01:22.000
or explain your setup later.

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:26.025
A homelab becomes more valuable when the environment can be recreated instead of merely

00:01:26.025 --> 00:01:26.430
remembered.
